Project: Aerial Image Object Detection for Drone Surveys
Overview
Built a computer-vision pipeline that detects buildings, vehicles, and trees from drone aerial imagery — nadir DJI captures, oblique shots, and large stitched panoramas (up to 14,400 × 7,200 px). The system benchmarks multiple deep-learning segmentation models head-to-head on the same imagery, and SegFormer‑B5 (ADE20K) was selected as the production model for delivering the cleanest, most accurate detections.

The Problem
Aerial/drone imagery is hard for off-the-shelf detectors: objects appear at varied scales and angles, panoramas are too large to feed into a model directly, and many models over-segment (e.g., counting one tree canopy as 10, or mistaking stones/debris as buildings). The goal was to find the model + post-processing combination that produces accurate, deduplicated, false-positive-free detections.

Approach — Multi-Model Benchmark
Instead of trusting one model, I built a benchmark harness (benchmark_panorama.py) that runs several segmentation models on identical images and compares per-class counts, speed, and visual quality side by side. Models evaluated:

Model	Backbone / Dataset	Classes	Source
SegFormer‑B5 ADE20K ✅ (selected)	MiT‑B5 / ADE20K	150	nvidia/segformer-b5-finetuned-ade-640-640
SegFormer‑B2 Cityscapes	MiT‑B2 / Cityscapes	19	nvidia/segformer-b2-finetuned-cityscapes-1024-1024
Aerial Drone SegFormer	SegFormer / drone data	24	Thalirajesh/Aerial-Drone-Image-Segmentation
SAM + CLIP (earlier approach)	Segment Anything + OpenAI CLIP	zero-shot	openai/clip-vit-base-patch32
Land-cover SegFormer (9 classes)	SegFormer / satellite	9	nave1616/SegFormer-landcover-FT
Why SegFormer‑B5 ADE20K won
Across the 11-image benchmark, the other models over-segmented — the Aerial-Drone model produced ~1,000 detections on a single panorama (683 "trees" alone), and Cityscapes also inflated counts, both generating large numbers of false positives. ADE20K's 150-class vocabulary lets it distinguish fine semantic categories (building / house / skyscraper / tower, car / bus / truck / van / minibike, tree / plant / palm), which translated into precise, well-bounded detections with far fewer false positives — e.g., on the same panorama it returned 338 clean detections vs. 1,000+ from the over-segmenting model. It became the production choice for best precision/accuracy, at a reasonable speed (~7–11 s per drone image, ~47–51 s per full panorama).

Engineering / Pipeline (the part that makes it work)
The model alone isn't enough — most of the accuracy comes from the surrounding pipeline I built in OpenCV/NumPy:

Sliding-window tiling — split giant panoramas into 1024 px tiles with 200 px overlap so the model sees objects at native resolution instead of downscaled mush.
Smart tile skipping — HSV-based sky detection + dark-tile rejection to avoid wasting inference on empty regions.
Semantic-mask → instance conversion — per-class binary masks cleaned with morphological open/close, then contour extraction + bounding boxes to turn segmentation into countable objects.
Building shape filter — rejects stones, debris, and cracks using rectangularity, solidity, compactness, and aspect-ratio geometry checks (buildings are rectangular/solid; debris is blobby/elongated).
Per-class Non-Maximum Suppression (IoU 0.3) — removes duplicate detections from overlapping tiles.
Output — annotated JPG with an on-image count table per class, plus a structured comparison.json for every image; also supports batch folders and drone video (frame-skip → annotated MP4).
